Kim: quick heads-up — I'm opening a docs PR to add a compact runbook for 'edge-client trimming + server fallthrough' behavior when clients hit 429s or context limits. PR link once created.
Devon: nice, can you include a short decision matrix? e.g. when to trim client-side vs switch model variant vs retry with backoff.
Kim: yeah — plan is 1) attempt client-side sliding-token trim 2) on 429 fallback to pinned smaller model variant (v1-small) with same prompt reduced, 3) record telemetry and surface guidance for product teams.
Marta: can we include a minimal SDK snippet? users keep asking for an example that shows trimming window + automatic fallback. For JS/Python both please.
Kim: putting a tiny JS snippet in the PR, something like:
```js
// client-side sliding-window trim + 429 fallback (pseudo)
async function generateWithTrim(client, prompt) {
  try {
    return await client.generate({ prompt });
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.code === 429) {
      const trimmed = trimFromFront(prompt, 1024); // keep last 1024 tokens
      return await client.generate({ prompt: trimmed, model: 'v1-small' });
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
```
Devon: good. Add a note about cost/latency tradeoffs of v1-small vs trimming. Also show an exponential backoff example for transient 429s.
Kim: will add backoff snippet and a short table mapping symptom -> recommended action (timeout vs context clip vs model fallback).
Ryan: I can review the layout and examples tomorrow. Also suggest we add a small synthetic repro script so docs QA can validate behavior — maybe a local rack that rate-limits to force 429.
Sam: I have a tiny harness used in support that simulates short bursts; happy to package it as `tools/429-sim` (MIT) and link it in the runbook.
Marta: also flagging that SDK docs need to mention token accounting: show how to estimate token counts before trimming and link to tokenizer utility. People forget and trim wrong side.
Kim: good catch — I'll add a short checklist: estimate tokens, try prefix-preserving trim, test locally with harness, then enable fallback to smaller model + telemetry events.
Devon: minor copy request — can we avoid telling users to always fallback to a smaller model automatically? prefer a canary/feature flag pattern so teams can opt-in.
Kim: absolutely — note will say "recommend feature-flagged rollout for automatic fallthrough".
Docs Bot: PR created by Kim — https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/4879 (draft).
Marta: thanks — I'll add the Python snippet and a quick link to the token-count utility in a separate commit.
Ryan: assign me as reviewer on the PR, I'll do a pass on examples + decision matrix Monday.
Sam: I'll send the harness README and a small replay script to Kim by EOD.
Kim: action items: Kim (author PR draft), Marta (python + token utility links), Ryan (review), Sam (harness + README), Devon (copy + rollout guidance). I'll mark in the PR description which files to touch: `runbooks/edge-client-trim.md`, `sdk-snippets/js/fallback-trim.md`, `sdk-snippets/python/fallback-trim.md`, and `tools/429-sim/README.md`.
Devon: perfect. One more ask — let's add a short troubleshooting checklist at top: "If users still see 429s after following runbook: contact infra oncall, include trimmed prompt, token counts, and recent request IDs."
Kim: will do. tagging this for follow-up next week. :white_check_mark:
